\subsubsection{Traffic Cop Router and Multi-Tier Domain Boundary Filtering}
Each query $q$ is first classified by the Traffic Cop Router, powered by Google Gemma 3 12B Instruction-tuned (\texttt{\RouterModel}) at $T=0.0$ under structured Pydantic validation (\texttt{RoutingDecision}):
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\text{Route}(q) = \begin{cases} 
\text{EASY}, & \begin{gathered}\text{Low Complexity}\\[-2pt]\land\ \text{In-Domain}\end{gathered} \\[6pt]
\text{HARD}, & \begin{gathered}\text{High Complexity}\\[-2pt]\lor\ \text{Out-of-Domain}\end{gathered}
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:routing}
\end{equation}
EASY queries (e.g., decision years or case authors) skip multi-hop expansion and go to the Grounded Fast Path, a $\AblationRouterLatencySlowdownFactor\times$ speedup in the pilot ablation (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}) that also avoids context bloat from tangential citing precedents. Evaluated standalone (Section~\ref{subsec:router-standalone-eval}), the router reaches $\RouterAccuracy$ accuracy and $\RouterMacroFOne$ macro-$F_1$ on the \numUniqueQueries-query suite.

\subsubsection{NLI Smart Grader and Self-Correction Expansion Loop}
\label{subsubsec:terra-nli-grader}
Before generation, $\mathcal{C}(q)$ is evaluated by the NLI Smart Grader, a structured-prompt call to \texttt{databricks-meta-llama-3-3-70b-instruct}~\cite{dubey2024llama3} ($T=0.0$) given the query and context and instructed to return JSON with \texttt{entails} (boolean), \texttt{confidence\_score} (float) and \texttt{reasoning} (string). This allows single-call verification without a separate NLI cross-encoder (e.g., DeBERTa~\cite{he2023debertav3}), while routing stays with the 12B Gemma SLM. No labeled validation set was built for the verifier; the only empirical evidence of its role is the pilot ablation, in which removing the gate (Ablation~2, Table~\ref{tab:ablation}) lowers adversarial safety rejection from \AblationFullCatDSafetyRate\ to \AblationTwoCatDSafetyRate, which is consistent with the gate contributing to safety but not a validation of its accuracy. False non-entailment (supported context rejected) and false entailment (insufficient context accepted) are unquantified failure modes (Section~\ref{subsec:limitations}). If \texttt{entails == True}, the context goes to the generator. If \texttt{entails == False}, a \textbf{Self-Correction Expansion Loop} widens retrieval ($k \leftarrow k + 2$), re-queries ChromaDB and re-runs BFS. The recovered HARD path permits two attempts in total (initial retrieval plus one fallback), not two retries; EASY has no expansion retry. Historical logs do not record intermediate retry transitions. If the permitted checks fail, the \textbf{Safety Refusal Firewall} halts generation with a deterministic refusal: \textit{``I apologize, but I do not have sufficient validated legal context in my databases to answer this query accurately without risking hallucination.''}

