\subsection{Adversarial Generalization Under Weak Parametric Memory: Obscure Overruled Precedents}
\label{subsec:obscure-generalization}
Table~\ref{tab:obscure_overruled_eval} reports the obscure suite (Section~\ref{subsec:benchmark-design}).
\input{tables/table_obscure_overruled_eval}
\paragraph{Parametric collapse.} Direct LLM safely handled \ObscDirectSafeSummary\ of the obscure queries, with \ObscDirectFatalAff\ answers affirming the overruled rule as current law and \ObscDirectFatalUnw\ reciting it without warning, compared with \ObscCrossDirectFamousSafe\ on the famous Category~D1 subset (descriptive Fisher $p\,\ObscCrossDirectFisherPRel$). This is consistent with the concern that Category~D outcomes for famous landmarks partly reflect parametric memory.
\paragraph{Preregistered primary test.} TERRA-Unlabeled versus Flat Vector RAG was not rejected (discordant pairs $b=\ObscPrimaryDiscordantB$, $c=\ObscPrimaryDiscordantC$; exact McNemar $p\,\ObscPrimaryMcNemarPRel$; gate \ObscPrimaryGate), so the fixed-sequence procedure leaves all secondary contrasts unconfirmed.
\paragraph{Source of the advantage.} Descriptively, Full TERRA exceeded TERRA-Unlabeled ($b=\ObscSecFullVsUnlabeledB$, $c=\ObscSecFullVsUnlabeledC$; exact $p\,\ObscSecFullVsUnlabeledPRel$). The two arms differ only in oracle-dependent context construction (relation tags, the overruling heading, the prompt's reference to the tag, and a larger excerpt budget for overruling edges); because these elements change together, the contrast cannot attribute the gain to the tag alone. We read the result as showing that TERRA's safety on obscure precedents depends on curated overruling annotations available to the system, not that unlabeled traversal fails: with $N=\ObscCohortSize$ the study cannot distinguish TERRA-Unlabeled from Flat Vector RAG.
\paragraph{Robustness.} The magnitude depends on the judge: Judge~1 scored Full TERRA at \ObscJudgeOneFullSafePct\ and TERRA-Unlabeled at \ObscJudgeOneUnlabeledSafePct\ (primary $p\,\ObscJudgeOnePrimaryPRel$), Judge~2 scored \ObscJudgeTwoFullSafePct\ and \ObscJudgeTwoUnlabeledSafePct\ (primary $p\,\ObscJudgeTwoPrimaryPRel$), and the combined severity rule tracks the stricter judge. The primary contrast is non-significant under both. Excluding items with empty TERRA context ($N=\ObscSubsetEmptyContextN$) gives primary $p\,\ObscSubsetEmptyContextPRel$ and Full TERRA \ObscSubsetEmptyContextFullSafePct; excluding items whose context lacks the overruler ($N=\ObscSubsetOverrulerMissingN$), $p\,\ObscSubsetOverrulerMissingPRel$ and \ObscSubsetOverrulerMissingFullSafePct; explicit-overrule items only ($N=\ObscSubsetExplicitOverruleN$), $p\,\ObscSubsetExplicitOverrulePRel$ and \ObscSubsetExplicitOverruleFullSafePct.
\paragraph{Cross-suite contrast (descriptive).} Full TERRA's safe handling was \ObscCrossFullObscureSafe\ on obscure precedents versus \ObscCrossFullFamousSafe\ on the famous subset (risk difference \ObscCrossFullRiskDiff, 95\% CI \ObscCrossFullNewcombeCI); Direct LLM and TERRA-Unlabeled fell from \ObscCrossDirectFamousSafe\ and \ObscCrossUnlabeledFamousSafe\ to \ObscCrossDirectObscureSafe\ and \ObscCrossUnlabeledObscureSafe. With $N=\ObscFamousCohortSize$ and $N=\ObscCohortSize$ these contrasts are descriptive; a non-significant difference does not establish invariance. The famous-subset rates use a stricter dual-judge rubric on nineteen items and are not comparable to the \TerraCatDSafeHandlingRate\ safe-handling rate of Table~\ref{tab:refusal_breakdown}.
\paragraph{Reliability.} Inter-judge agreement was Cohen's $\kappa=\ObscKappa$ over \ObscKappaCleanEvals\ judgments; evidence quotes could not be verified verbatim for \ObscQwenUnverifiedQuotes\ (Judge~1) and \ObscGptOssUnverifiedQuotes\ (Judge~2) judgments, with labels retained. Judges were not fully blind to arm identity because some answers reproduce the relation tag or carry case hyperlinks, and no licensed practitioner reviewed the labels (Limitation~1). A blinded spot-check by one author (not a licensed practitioner) of \ObscSpotN\ items stratified by judge disagreement, primary-test discordance and random selection matched the combined class on \ObscSpotAgreeMatch\ of \ObscSpotAgreeN\ items where the judges agreed but on only \ObscSpotDisagreeMatch\ of \ObscSpotDisagreeN\ where they disagreed. All \ObscSpotMismatchN\ combined-class mismatches rated the answer safer than the severity rule did (Full TERRA \ObscSpotMismatchFull, Flat Vector RAG \ObscSpotMismatchFlat, Direct LLM \ObscSpotMismatchDirect, TERRA-Unlabeled \ObscSpotMismatchUnlabeled), so the rule was more severe on those mismatches, but not uniformly across arms, which may affect arm contrasts; the check is not a validation of either judge.

